   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the criminal procedure law, the family court act and the executive law, in relation to improve the integrity of statements of those accused of crimes and the integrity of eyewitness identifications, to enhance criminal investigations and prosecutions and to promote confidence in the criminal justice system of this state",
    "basePrintNo": "S5875",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5875-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 59,
       "fullName": "Patrick M. Gallivan",
       "imgName": "427_patrick_m._gallivan.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 427,
       "sessionMemberId": 696,
       "sessionYear": 2015,
       "shortName": "GALLIVAN"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§60.45, 60.25, 60.30, 710.20 & 710.30, CP L; amd §§344.2, 343.3 & 343.4, Fam Ct Act; amd §§837 & 840, Exec L",
    "lawSection": "Criminal Procedure Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5875\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the criminal procedure law, the family\ncourt act and the executive law, in relation to improve the integrity\nof statements of those accused of crimes and the integrity of\neyewitness identifications, to enhance criminal investigations and\nprosecutions and to promote confidence in the criminal justice system\nof this state\n\nPURPOSE: To promote fairness and efficiency to the parties in the\nadjudication of criminal matters,\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 Amends the Criminal Procedure Law and requires law\nenforcement officials to video record statements made by accused\npersons in custody at a detention facility for certain violent\nfelonies and provides procedures therefore, and exceptions thereto\nunder certain circumstances.\n\nSection 2 Amends the Family Court Act and requires a public servant to\nvideo record statements made by a respondent child in custody in\naccordance with certain sections of 60.45 of the Criminal Procedure\nLaw.\n\nSection 3 Amends the Criminal Procedure Law allowing testimony under\nlimited certain circumstances by another person as to a prior\nidentification of the defendant made by a witness who is unable by\npresent recollection to identify a defendant\n\nSection 4 Amends the Criminal Procedure Law to permit the use of\npictorial, photographic, electronic, or video recorded reproduction of\nan identification procedure to be used in addition to present\nidentification by a witness of a defendant as evidence in chief at\ntrial under certain limited circumstances\n\nSection 5 Amends the Criminal Procedure Law providing that failure to\ncomply with certain provisions of blind or blinded identification\nprocureds is not in and of itself legally sufficient to suppress such\nidentification procedure.\n\nSection 6 Amends Criminal Procedure incorporating the reforms of this\nBill into notice requirements People have at arraignment of the\ndefendant regarding identification procedures.\n\nSections 7 & 8 Amend Family Court Act incorporating the above\nIdentification reforms into family court.\n\nSections 9 & 10 Amend the Executive Law requiring DCJS to formulate\nbest practices and procedures for law enforcement to implement the\nabove reforms.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:  On June 2, 2015, David P. Miranda, President, and\nGlenn Lau-Kee, Immediate Past President of the New York State Bar\nAssociation, Frank A. Sedita III, Erie County District Attorney and\nPresident of the District Attorneys Association of the State of New\nYork, and Barry Scheck, Co-Founder of the Innocence Project reached a\n\n\nlong-sought agreement on a process to require the recording of\ncustodial interrogations in certain serious crimes and to allow the\nadmissibility of photographic arrays when enhanced identification\nprocedures are used. This legislation, based on the proposal submitted\nby those organizations to the Legislature and the Governor's office,\nembodies the agreement.\n\nRecording of the custodial interrogation of a suspect and requiring\nblind or double-blind identification procedures when asking a victim\nor a witness to identify a suspect, are best practices that would\nprotect the innocent and enhance the capability of law enforcement to\napprehend and convict the guilty. These reforms will improve public\ntrust and confidence in our criminal justice system.\n\nThe proposal seeks to reduce two of the largest contributing factors\nto wrongful conviction: eyewitness misidentification, and false\nconfession.\n\nThe enactment of this single proposal in its entirety will greatly\nstrengthen New York's criminal justice system by promoting effective\nand uniform law enforcement procedures thereby improving public\nsafety, and helping to address some of the root causes of wrongful\nconvictions  This legislation builds upon statewide procedures already\nvoluntarily adopted by New York's Police and District Attorneys but\nnow makes many of them required standard practice, It is another step\nforward in New York's commitment to ensure that the innocent remain\nfree and that the guilty are not free to commit more crimes.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY: New Bill\n\nFISCAL IMPACT: None for fiscal year 2015-16.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:  With respect to mandatory recording of custodial\ninterrogations, this act shall take effect on June 1, 2017 and apply\nto confessions, admissions or statements made on or after such\neffective date\n\nWith respect to eyewitness identification procedures the bill shall\ntake effect on November 1, 2015.\n\n",
